{"reference":"Job 35","book":"Job","chapter":35,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"In the Wider Book","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job 35 belongs to Elihu’s four-speech sequence, which begins only after Job and the three friends have stopped speaking and ends before the divine answer. Elihu says he had held back because of his youth and speaks angrily toward both Job and the friends. Readers differ over his role: some see him as preparing the way for God’s response, while others see him as another inadequate defender of retribution or as a later literary intervention. His absence from the prologue, the divine address, and the epilogue’s rebuke does not by itself settle the question.","confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_035_6989befaed1f993d53f43c25"]},{"id":"block_2","text":"A later reference to Job connects him with suffering, patience, endurance, and the Lord’s compassionate outcome. In that wider use, Job’s steadfastness can describe persistence or the story’s outcome; it should not be read as requiring silent endurance of abuse or ruling out escape and protection.","confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_035_f24a8f7a95a2e5be0ad412e8"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}